WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.600
Hardening starts with knowing what exists.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:08.020
A useful inventory includes routers, switches, firewalls, wireless controllers,

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:15.520
VPN gateways, servers, appliances, cloud networks, subnets, VLANs, DNS and DHCP services,

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:19.820
NTP sources, third-party connections, and management interfaces.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:24.680
It should also identify what each asset does, where it lives, what it depends on,

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.580
and whether it is still supported.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.120
Unknown assets are hard to patch, hard to monitor, and hard to protect.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.820
Ownership matters as much as discovery.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.160
Every network segment, rule set, service,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.260
and connection should have a responsible team or person.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.760
If a firewall rule allows traffic between a user subnet and a server network,

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.820
someone should understand why it exists and who can approve changes.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:54.600
If a VPN profile grants administrative access, someone should own the access review.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:59.540
Without ownership, hardening questions become slow and risky during production pressure.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.720
Network maps do not need to be perfect on day one,

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.660
but they must be accurate enough to support decisions.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:11.800
A practical map shows critical paths: user networks, server networks, management networks,

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:17.060
guest wireless, cloud networks, internet edges, firewalls, remote access paths,

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.120
and key dependencies.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.420
It should help answer operational questions such as what depends on this subnet,

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.560
what would break if this rule changed, and where monitoring should exist.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.860
Inventory is not a static spreadsheet.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.240
It should be reviewed when projects launch, systems retire, sites change,

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.980
cloud networks are added, or emergency exceptions are created.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.480
A network that was accurate last quarter may already have drifted.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.900
Regular review turns hardening from guesswork into managed operations and gives teams a

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.440
safer starting point for exposure reduction,

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:52.780
segmentation, patching, and validation.
